course: world
lesson: 179
title: "It's natural so it's healthy" and the miracle cure
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: "It's natural, so it's good for you" sounds kind and cosy, but nature also makes deadly nightshade, poisonous mushrooms and snake venom. Whether something is safe depends on what it is and how much, not on where it came from.
explain: "Miracle" cures work the same way: a cream that removed every wrinkle in three days would be front-page news, not a pop-up advert. If it sounds too good to be true, it usually is.
explain: Watch out for "it worked for me": colds get better in about a week anyway, and Uncle Mo started physiotherapy the same week he bought his magnet bracelet, so the bracelet got the credit for the exercises.
explain: Ask the dull questions: Which toxins? Who tested it? Compared with what? "Big companies are hiding it" is not proof; it is an excuse for having no proof.
explain: Grandma gets it right when she laughs, "Miracle cream my foot, the only thing it removed was twenty quid from my purse": she laughs at the claim, and at herself.
fact: Lots of natural things are poisonous, such as some mushrooms, deadly nightshade and snake venom, so "natural" does not mean "safe".
fact: Herbal pills can affect the body like other medicines and can do harm, so ask a pharmacist (the medicines expert at the chemist's) before taking one.
fact: No cream removes all wrinkles in three days; a real breakthrough would be big news, not a pop-up advert.
fact: Studies have found that magnetic bracelets don't help pain more than fake ones, while physiotherapy (exercises and stretches taught by a trained physiotherapist, or "physio") really can help back pain.
fact: Doctors want people to be healthy, so a headline saying "doctors hate this one trick" is clickbait, made to get you to click.
fact: Honey is natural but mostly sugar, and it can cause tooth decay like other sugar; babies under one year old should not have honey at all.
fact: Most colds get better in 7 to 10 days with or without any "cure", which is why so many cures seem to work.
fact: Your liver and kidneys remove waste from your body every day; detox teas don't have good evidence behind them.
fact: When a cure works, scientists test it and publish the results; "they're hiding it" is not evidence that it works.
fact: Organic farming follows rules such as using fewer artificial chemicals and can be better for some soil and animals, but an organic apple has about the same vitamins as an ordinary one.
fact: Famous people are often paid to say they use a product; a famous face is not evidence that it works.
fact: Words people use: "quid" (UK) means pounds; "my foot!" means "rubbish!"; "bling" means shiny jewellery; "my guy" means "mate"; "dodgy" means not to be trusted; "grand" (Ireland) means fine; "scoff" (UK) means eat greedily; "lowkey" means quietly, a bit.
exercise: Auntie Joy says about a herbal pill she bought online, "It's natural, so it's good for you." What is wrong? | Many natural things are harmful, so natural doesn't mean safe | Natural things are safe in any amount at all | Only pills made abroad are ever dangerous | Herbal pills can't affect the body in any way
exercise: Which of these is natural AND poisonous? | Deadly nightshade | Carrots | Apples | Rice
exercise: Before taking a herbal pill, who is the best person to ask? | A pharmacist | The website that sells it | A friend who likes it | Nobody, since it's natural
exercise: "This miracle cream removes all wrinkles in three days!" Grandma buys it; three days later, same face. What is the lesson? | If it sounds too good to be true, it usually is | She should have used it for four days, then it works | Miracle creams only work on young people | The cream works, but only on TV
exercise: Uncle Mo's magnet bracelet "cured" his back the same week he started seeing a physiotherapist. What probably helped? | The physiotherapy exercises | The strong magnet in the bracelet | Wearing anything shiny on the wrist | The bright colour of the bracelet
exercise: What have studies found about magnetic bracelets and pain? | They help no more than fake ones | They cure back pain in one week | They work only on the left wrist | They work better than any medicine
exercise: A website says, "Doctors HATE this one trick to lose weight!" Why is Ollie's mum suspicious? | Doctors would welcome anything that really helped people | Doctors really do hate people losing weight | Only doctors are allowed to read websites | Tricks never work for anybody
exercise: A friend says, "Sugar is fine if it's from honey, because honey is natural." What is true? | Honey is mostly sugar and can rot teeth too | Honey has no sugar in it at all | Teeth only rot from sugar made in factories | Honey cleans teeth just like toothpaste
exercise: Who should not have honey at all? | Babies under one year old | Children over five years old | Grandparents | People who like sweets
exercise: Grandad's friend: "A spoonful of vinegar a day cured my cold in a week." What is the likely truth? | Colds usually get better in about a week anyway | Vinegar kills every cold virus in the body within days | Colds last for months without vinegar | Vinegar works only when mixed with lemon
exercise: A shop sells "detox tea" that "removes all toxins". Which toxins? The shop doesn't know. What already cleans your blood? | The liver and kidneys | The tea and the cup | The skin and the hair | The teeth and the tongue
exercise: A video says, "Big companies don't want you to know this natural cure!" What is wrong with it? | It uses the lack of proof as if it were proof | It gives the full scientific results | It shows the cure was tested on many people for years | It names the scientists who checked it
exercise: What happens when a cure really works? | Scientists test it and publish the results | It stays a secret for ever, so nobody can check | Only one video on the internet ever talks about it | It can only ever be bought online, from one shop
exercise: Aunt Sophie: "Organic food is healthier because it's grown naturally." What is true? | An organic apple has about the same vitamins as an ordinary one | Organic food always has double the vitamins | An organic doughnut counts as a health food | Organic means grown with no soil at all
exercise: A woman on TV says a famous actress uses her cream: "If it's good enough for a star, it's good enough for you." What is wrong? | The star may be paid, and fame isn't evidence | Stars only ever use cheap creams | Famous people can't have skin problems, so it works | Nothing; it's true for every product
test: Teen: "Bro, this herbal pill is all-natural, it's lowkey a health hack fr." What is the reply? | Natural isn't the same as safe; ask a pharmacist | All-natural pills can't harm anyone, so it's fine | Health hacks always come from nature | Pills are safe if they're sold online
test: Grandma laughs: "Miracle cream, my foot! The only thing it removed was twenty quid." What is she doing well? | Laughing at the false claim, and at herself | Planning to buy two more jars to give it a chance | Blaming her own face for not changing in time | Deciding that all creams are miracles after all
test: What would happen if a cream really removed every wrinkle in three days? | It would be tested and become huge news | Doctors would hide it for ever from everyone | Only one small website would ever know about it | It would stay a pop-up advert on dodgy sites
test: Uncle Mo: "It's the magnet, 100%. Physio's just stretching." His wife: "Mo, the magnet's just bling." Who is right? | His wife; studies show magnets don't beat fake ones | Uncle Mo; magnets pull the pain right out of his back muscles | Both; magnets and physio work equally well | Neither; nothing ever helps back pain
test: In a cartoon a hero drinks "detox tea" and green smoke labelled TOXINS puffs out of his ears. Put the cartoon right. | Your liver and kidneys clean the body; detox teas lack evidence | Detox tea really pushes toxins out through your ears | Toxins are green smoke stored inside your head | Only cartoon heroes have livers and kidneys
test: Bill in Dublin: "Vinegar sorted my cold in a week, I'm grand now." What would most likely have happened without the vinegar? | The cold would have gone in about a week anyway | The cold would have lasted all winter, maybe longer | Bill would have caught two more colds | Bill would have needed to go to hospital
test: "Doctors HATE this one trick 😱" on a dodgy website. What is the best reading? | It's clickbait, made to get you to click | Doctors are hiding the best trick ever | Any website with a shocked emoji is true | Doctors only hate tricks that work
test: Teen: "So the proof it works is that there's no proof? That's not how proof works, my guy." What is the teen pointing out? | "They're hiding it" can't stand in for evidence | Every real cure has to be kept secret by the companies | Proof isn't needed for natural cures | Big companies make every video online
test: Mate: "Honey's natural innit, so I can scoff loads and get no fillings." What is true? | Honey is mostly sugar and can cause tooth decay | Honey can't harm teeth at all, because it's natural | Honey is fine as long as it's runny | Natural sugar fills holes in teeth
test: Nephew: "An organic doughnut is still a doughnut, Auntie." What is he saying? | A label doesn't make a food healthy | Organic food is always unhealthy for you | Doughnuts can never be organic, by law | Aunts shouldn't be the ones buying food
test: An advert: "A famous actress uses this cream!" Mum: "She's got a chef, a trainer and twelve hours' sleep. It's not the cream." What is Mum's point? | Her looks may come from other things, and she may be paid | The cream must work twice as well on her | Actresses never use any creams | Chefs are the ones who make face creams
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the claim that a substance is beneficial because it is natural? | Natural origin does not establish safety, since many natural substances are poisonous | Natural substances are harmless in any quantity | Only manufactured substances may cause harm | Natural origin guarantees a substance's effectiveness
exam: It is given that an individual recovered from a common cold within one week of commencing a daily spoonful of vinegar. Identify the principal reason for which this does not demonstrate that vinegar is a cure. | Common colds ordinarily resolve within seven to ten days without treatment | Vinegar is known to prolong the common cold | One week is insufficient for any recovery | Common colds do not resolve without medication
exam: Ascertain which organs of the human body are responsible for the removal of waste from the blood on a daily basis. | The liver and the kidneys | The lungs and the heart | The stomach and the skin | The bones and the muscles
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the assertion that a cure is concealed by large companies and is therefore genuine. | The alleged concealment constitutes no evidence of effectiveness | The alleged concealment proves that the cure is effective and safe | Effective cures are invariably concealed | Published trials are invariably false
